The purpose of the EXTENDED SERVICE REQUEST message is to support additional service request use cases beyond basic packet services, including:

- CS fallback or 1xCS fallback (mobile terminating, mobile originating, emergency);
- NAS signalling connection release;
- Rejection of paging;
- Removal of paging restriction;
- Handling of MUSIM-specific requests (e.g., paging restriction negotiation);
- Signalling for SIPTO/LIPA-related access changes and bearer context management;
- Indicating EPS bearer context status for local deactivation by the network.

It extends the functionality of the SERVICE REQUEST message by carrying additional information elements (e.g., CSFB response IE, UE request type IE, Paging restriction IE, EPS bearer context status IE) required for these advanced procedures — as mandated across clauses 5.6.1.1 cases d, e, f, g, i, j, o, p, q, and for low-priority NAS signalling when network support is indicated.

The UE shall include the Device properties IE when it is configured for NAS signalling low priority and needs to indicate this configuration, i.e., by setting the low priority indicator to "MS is configured for NAS signalling low priority" — **except** in the following cases where it shall instead set the low priority indicator to "MS is not configured for NAS signalling low priority":

- the UE is performing an attach for emergency bearer services;  
- the UE has a PDN connection for emergency bearer services established and is performing EPS mobility management procedures, or is establishing a PDN connection for emergency bearer services;  
- the UE configured for dual priority is requested by the upper layers to establish a PDN connection with the low priority indicator set to "MS is not configured for NAS signalling low priority";  
- the UE configured for dual priority is performing EPS session management procedures related to the PDN connection established with low priority indicator set to "MS is not configured for NAS signalling low priority";  
- the UE configured for dual priority has a PDN connection established by setting the low priority indicator to "MS is not configured for NAS signalling low priority" and is performing EPS mobility management procedures;  
- the UE is performing a service request procedure for a CS fallback emergency call or 1xCS fallback emergency call;  
- the UE is a UE configured to use AC11 15 in selected PLMN; or  
- the UE is responding to paging.

This is explicitly stated in the context:  
> *"A UE configured for NAS signalling low priority [...] indicates this by including the Device properties IE in the appropriate NAS message and setting the low priority indicator to 'MS is configured for NAS signalling low priority', except for the following cases in which the UE shall set the low priority indicator to 'MS is not configured for NAS signalling low priority'"* — followed by the list above.

This clause describes the UE’s behaviour related to the **back-off timer for the bearer resource modification procedure**, including:

- When and how the UE shall start, deactivate, or ignore the back-off timer upon receiving a **BEARER RESOURCE MODIFICATION REJECT** message;
- Dependencies on the **ESM cause value** (e.g., #26, #32, #33) and presence/absence of the **Back-off timer value IE** and **Re-attempt indicator IE**;
- Use of configured values (**SM_RetryWaitTime**, **SM_RetryAtRATChange**) from ME or USIM, or fallback to default (e.g., 12 minutes);
- Behaviour across **PLMN changes**, **inter-system changes** (e.g., S1 ↔ A/Gb/Iu ↔ N1 mode), and **equivalent PLMN list** considerations;
- Distinction between timers scoped to specific access types (S1 only vs. A/Gb/Iu/N1) based on network indication;
- Clarification that the back-off timer is a *logical model*, not mandating use of a specific timer (e.g., T3396 is optional implementation choice);
- That the timer persists across PLMN/inter-system changes unless deactivated, and is stopped only on UE power-off or USIM removal.

In summary: **UE back-off timer handling for bearer resource modification — activation conditions, scope, persistence, and inter-RAT/PLMN implications.**

The table in each definition lists:

- The Information Elements (IE) known in the message and the order of their appearance in the message.  
- For each information element, it indicates:  
  1. The Information Element Identifier (IEI), in hexadecimal notation (if applicable);  
  2. The name of the information element;  
  3. The name of the type of the information element (and generally the referenced clause describing its value part);  
  4. The presence requirement indication (M, C, or O);  
  5. The format of the information element (T, V, TV, LV, TLV, LV-E or TLV-E);  
  6. The length of the information element (or permissible range of lengths), in octets, where "?" means the maximum length is only constrained by the link layer protocol (non-normative).

Examples of the received information that influences EMM common procedure initiation include:

- **ATTACH REQUEST** message (e.g., triggering EMM authentication if received before SERVICE REJECT or ATTACH COMPLETE, or in EMM-REGISTERED state);  
- **TRACKING AREA UPDATE REQUEST** message (e.g., triggering EMM authentication if received before SERVICE REJECT or before ATTACH COMPLETE);  
- **SERVICE REQUEST**, **EXTENDED SERVICE REQUEST**, or **CONTROL PLANE SERVICE REQUEST** messages (e.g., leading to aborting the ongoing service request and initiating EMM common procedures upon concurrent ATTACH or TAU);  
- **Invalid or unacceptable UE security capabilities** (e.g., all-zero EPS encryption/integrity algorithm bits) — though this typically leads to ATTACH REJECT, it may trigger EMM common procedures (e.g., authentication) in some contexts per implementation;  
- **Protocol errors** in mandatory/conditional IEs (e.g., cause #96, #99, #100, #111) — while these usually trigger rejection, they are part of the received information that determines whether EMM common procedures are initiated or aborted.

These are explicitly cited in the context as inputs that condition network behavior regarding EMM common procedure initiation (clauses d, e, j, and abnormal case handling for ATTACH/SERVICE/TUA).
